y"},{"label":"GVA per head","value":16646,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"GDP per head","value":20334,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D council tax (area bill)","value":2705.33,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D \u2014 council's own element","value":277.46,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"}]}},"dossier":{"summary":null,"compliance":null,"documents":[],"read_documents":[],"calendar":null,"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"\u00a314.9m investment property portfolio generates surplus rental income relied upon to support revenue budget sustainability","lead":"Revenue budget sustainability depends on sustained rental income from investment properties; disruption to tenancies, income streams, or market conditions directly impacts council's ability to service \u00a325m debt and maintain service delivery","iso":"2026-03-24","paper_title":"Appendix A \u2013 Capital Strategy 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s35791/35791.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Council scheduled to cease 31 March 2028 under Local Government Reorganisation, creating governance uncertainty over two-year transition","lead":"Organisational dissolution creates uncertainty about debt serviceability, asset ownership, and liabilities continuity affecting credit assessment and covenant compliance","iso":"2026-03-24","paper_title":"Appendix A \u2013 Capital Strategy 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s35791/35791.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Council debt of \u00a324.96m represents 242% of core spending power with unearmarked reserves at only 19.2% of net revenue spend","lead":"High debt-to-income ratios combined with thin reserves indicate vulnerability to revenue shocks or income disruptions","iso":"2026-03-24","paper_title":"Appendix A \u2013 Capital Strategy 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s35791/35791.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-03-24","committee":"West Devon Council","paper_title":"Appendix A \u2013 Capital Strategy 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s35791/35791.pdf","headline":"\u00a314.9m investment property portfolio generates surplus rental income relied upon to support revenue budget sustainability","detail":"Council holds investment properties valued at \u00a314.905m as at 31 March 2025. Investment Strategy explicitly requires tenant rental income to exceed PWLB debt-servicing costs, with surplus directed to revenue budget. Council maintains Maintenance, Management and Risk Mitigation Reserve at 15% of all rental income, with balance exceeding \u00a3890,000 as at 31 March 2026.","category":"income","matters":"Revenue budget sustainability depends on sustained rental income from investment properties; disruption to tenancies, income streams, or market conditions directly impacts council's ability to service \u00a325m debt and maintain service delivery","figures":"\u00a314.9m investment portfolio value, 15% maintenance reserve requirement, reserve balance \u00a3890,000+","quote":"Rental income paid by the tenant must exceed the cost of repaying the borrowed money from the Public Works Loan Board (which is itself funded by the Government). The surplus is then an ancillary benefit which supports the Council's budget position and enables the Council to continue to provide services for local people.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-03-24","committee":"West Devon Council","paper_title":"Appendix A \u2013 Capital Strategy 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s35791/35791.pdf","headline":"Council scheduled to cease 31 March 2028 under Local Government Reorganisation, creating governance uncertainty over two-year transition","detail":"West Devon Borough Council will cease to exist 31 March 2028 as part of LGR. Strategy documents continue to 2028/29 'for modelling and illustration purposes' only, but paper does not articulate how \u00a324.96m debt, \u00a314.9m investment properties, or \u00a39.7m reserves will transition to successor authority or how long-term borrowing obligations will be discharged.","category":"governance","matters":"Organisational dissolution creates uncertainty about debt serviceability, asset ownership, and liabilities continuity affecting credit assessment and covenant compliance","figures":"Council dissolution 31 March 2028, current debt \u00a324.96m, investment properties \u00a314.9m, total reserves \u00a39.7m","quote":"The current timetable for Local Government Reorganisation (LGR) is such that the District Council would cease on 31 st March 2028, for modelling and illustration purposes the 2028/29 financial year has continued to be shown.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-03-24","committee":"West Devon Council","paper_title":"Appendix A \u2013 Capital Strategy 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s35791/35791.pdf","headline":"Council debt of \u00a324.96m represents 242% of core spending power with unearmarked reserves at only 19.2% of net revenue spend","detail":"As at 31 January 2026, external borrowing is \u00a325.9m. Total debt of \u00a324.96m is 242% of core spending power (\u00a310.31m). Unearmarked reserves of \u00a31.9m represent only 19.2% of net revenue spend (\u00a39.89m). Annual debt-servicing costs are \u00a31.44m, consuming 14.6% of net revenue in 2026/27, leaving limited margin for revenue shortfalls.","category":"borrowing","matters":"High debt-to-income ratios combined with thin reserves indicate vulnerability to revenue shocks or income disruptions","figures":"\u00a324.96m total debt, 242% of core spending power, unearmarked reserves \u00a31.9m (19.2% of net revenue), debt-service costs \u00a31.44m (14.6% of net revenue)","quote":"Appendix H to the Budget Proposals report for 2026/27 (Council 17th February 2026) sets out the following financial metrics: - Unearmarked Reserves as % of net revenue spend - \u00a31,900,000/ \u00a39,892,995 = 19.2% - Total debt as a % of core spending power - \u00a324,955,700 / \u00a310,314,000 = 242%","material":true},{"iso":"2026-03-24","committee":"West Devon Council","paper_title":"Appendix A \u2013 Capital Strategy 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s35791/35791.pdf","headline":"Okehampton Interchange Railway Station budget increased by \u00a33.2m mid-project to \u00a318.34m total","detail":"West Devon's \u00a313.4m Levelling Up bid for railway station and transport hub has seen capital budget increased by \u00a33.2m (total \u00a318.34m). Cost revision made by Devon County Council in 2025 reflecting current market rates and adjustment of COVID-era estimates. Project expected completion May 2026, funded from S106 contributions received by Devon County Council.","category":"capital","matters":"Cost escalation on major infrastructure projects indicates inflation risk and project control issues affecting capital planning and borrowing requirements","figures":"\u00a33.2m increase, total \u00a318.34m (originally \u00a313.4m Levelling Up funding), May 2026 completion","quote":"The capital budget for the railway station has been increased by \u00a33.2million (total \u00a318.34m), which is funded from S106 contributions received by DCC. The station's cost estimate was revised by DCC in 2025 to reflect current market rates and to reflect the fact that the original estimates were produced during the COVID pandemic.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-03-24","committee":"West Devon Council","paper_title":"2026/27 Capital Strategy, 2026/27 Treasury Management Strategy and 2026/27 Investment Strategy","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s35790/35790.pdf","headline":"Investment income budget increased to \u00a31.1 million for 2026/27","detail":"Treasury and investment income is budgeted at \u00a31.1 million, up from \u00a3950,000 in 2025/26 \u2014 a 15.8% increase. This represents material revenue contribution to council finances from cash and investment management.","category":"income","matters":"Investment income contributes to budget sustainability; an upward trend supports debt service capacity and financial resilience, but relies on continued interest rate environment and asset valuations","figures":"\u00a31.1 million budgeted for 2026/27; \u00a3950,000 in 2025/26","quote":"The budget for investment income has increased for 2026/27 and has been set at \u00a31.1m (this was \u00a3950,000 in 2025/26).","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-03-24"},"name":"West Devon","findings_total":43,"news_drafts_total":3}}